.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt MORIARTY\-PROJECT\-TOOLS 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm moriarty\-project\-tools
.Nd project commands and checks configuration for moriarty
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The project file
.Pa .config/tools.toml
configures commands for the project\-tools MCP server and checks for that server
and the Claude Code Stop hook.
It is distinct from
.Pa ~/.config/moriarty/tool_rules.toml ,
which controls tool call permissions rather than defining project commands.
.Pp
Create the file in the workspace root, then review and approve its commands with
.Bd -literal -offset indent
moriarty approve\-project .
.Ed
Approval is required before configured commands or checks execute.
The file does not install tools; its programs must already be available.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The file uses TOML.
The
.Ic [commands]
table is required, even for a checks\-only configuration, where it may be empty.
Each command is optional; omitted commands are unavailable rather than given a
default.
The
.Ic [[checks]]
array of tables is optional.
.Pp
Command values are non\-empty arrays of strings.
The first string names the executable; remaining strings are separate arguments.
Moriarty executes the program directly, not through a shell:
quoting, variable expansion, pipelines, and redirects inside arguments are not
interpreted.
Use an executable script for multi\-step operations.
.Ss Commands
The four supported keys in
.Ic [commands]
map to fixed MCP tools:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic lint
Runs through
.Cm run_lint .
.It Ic test
Runs through
.Cm run_tests .
.It Ic build
Runs through
.Cm run_build .
.It Ic format
Runs through
.Cm run_formatter .
.El
.Pp
Arbitrary command keys do not define new MCP tools.
Unknown configuration keys are currently ignored, so check spelling carefully.
.Ss Checks
Each
.Ic [[checks]]
entry requires:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic name
A string identifying the check in results and approvals.
Names must be unique across checks; duplicate names reject the configuration.
.It Ic command
A non\-empty executable\-and\-arguments array, using the same format as
command values.
.El
.Pp
Checks are independent of commands: defining
.Ic commands.test
does not automatically run it at the Stop hook.
Add a check explicitly for each validation that should run there.
There are no per\-check environment, working\-directory, or timeout fields.
.Sh EXECUTION
.Ss Configuration and Program Paths
The configuration is loaded from the invoking workspace root:
the nearest ancestor containing
.Pa .jj/repo ,
otherwise the git worktree root, otherwise the canonical project directory.
Each jj secondary workspace or git worktree therefore uses its own file.
This project\-relative path does not follow
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.Pp
Absolute program paths are used as given.
Relative program paths containing a slash, such as
.Pa ./scripts/check\-policy.sh ,
are resolved against the workspace root.
Bare program names are looked up through
.Ev PATH .
Execution uses the resolved program path verified during approval;
other relative arguments are interpreted by the program in its execution
directory.
.Ss MCP Commands and CLI Tests
Start the MCP server with
.Cm moriarty mcp project\-tools .
Its tools accept an optional
.Ic project_dir
argument, defaulting to the server's current directory.
The four command tools execute in the caller's canonical project directory.
.Pp
.Cm moriarty test project\-tools Op Ar project_dir
runs all configured commands;
.Cm moriarty test checks Op Ar project_dir
runs all configured checks.
Both default to the current directory and execute there after canonicalization.
These CLI runners and the four MCP command tools verify
.Em all
configured commands and checks before execution, even when running only one
command.
Parallel CLI runs allow at most four simultaneous processes, with no timeout
or output caps.
.Ss Stop Hook and MCP Checks
The Stop hook and the MCP
.Cm run_checks
tool share a checks\-only runner.
They verify checks, not the four commands, and execute at the workspace root.
Checks run with at most four simultaneous processes, a five\-minute timeout for
the entire run, a 1 MiB output limit per check, and a 10 MiB aggregate output
limit.
These limits are built in, not configurable in this file.
.Pp
A failed check, execution error, approval failure, or exceeded limit blocks
the run.
No checks means no validation is performed.
A missing or otherwise unusable configuration also skips validation, except that
duplicate check names explicitly block it.
The MCP command tools and CLI test runners instead report configuration
loading errors.
.Sh APPROVALS
Run
.Cm moriarty approve\-project Ar workspace_root
after adding or changing commands or checks.
The TUI reviews configured items, their resolved programs, and security details;
approvals are saved separately in
.Pa ~/.config/moriarty/project_approvals.toml
.Pq under Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME No when set .
.Pp
Approvals bind each item's argument array, original and canonical program paths,
and program SHA\-256 hash.
An item may have multiple approved versions; its current definition must match
an approved version.
A new name, changed arguments, changed program path, or changed binary requires
approval when no stored version matches.
Cosmetic TOML changes and removing items do not require re\-approval.
.Pp
Approvals are keyed by the shared repository root, not the workspace directory.
Workspace\-local program paths are stored relative to the workspace root, so
byte\-identical tools with identical arguments in another worktree can share
approval.
The file and binaries still come from that worktree, not the main workspace.
.Pp
Approval verifies the executable, not every file it might read or run.
For example, approving a shell interpreter does not hash a script passed as an
argument.
Prefer a directly executable script when its contents should be bound to
approval.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa <workspace>/.config/tools.toml
Project commands and checks.
.It Pa ~/.config/moriarty/project_approvals.toml
User approval state, respecting
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A project with all four commands and two Stop\-hook checks:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[commands]
lint = ["cargo", "clippy", "\-\-all\-targets", "\-\-", "\-D", "warnings"]
test = ["cargo", "nextest", "run"]
build = ["cargo", "build"]
format = ["cargo", "fmt"]

[[checks]]
name = "format"
command = ["cargo", "fmt", "\-\-", "\-\-check"]

[[checks]]
name = "tests"
command = ["cargo", "nextest", "run"]
.Ed
.Pp
A checks\-only configuration using a workspace\-local executable script:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[commands]

[[checks]]
name = "policy"
command = ["./scripts/check\-policy.sh"]
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr moriarty 1 ,
.Xr moriarty\-bash\-rules 5 ,
.Xr moriarty\-tool\-rules 5
